I was having a similar problem where parboot was not giving me confidence intervals. I was pretty confused about this for a while, but I figured out what I think is a simple work-around.
Objects made with parboot have a value called "t.star" (in addition to "call" and "t0") that returns a vector of the simulation fits. If you did 100 simulations (nsim = 100), this vector will be 100 values long.
I used the parboot t.star value, along with the "quantile" function to get a 95% confidence interval for the set of simulation fits. It worked out the following way, where "pbFall" is my parboot object:

> quantile(pbF...@t.star, probs = c(0.025, 0.975))
    2.5%    97.5%
151.3983 237.6800

I hope this answered your question!
Michael

