Here's a more detailed response.  Consider modifying the parameterization of
the model so that there are separate parameters for PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND = 0
and PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND = 1.

  m5a <- colext(
    data = BLSA_UMF,
    psiformula = ~ 0 + as.factor(PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND),

    epsilonformula = ~ 1,
    pformula = ~ 1,

    control = list(trace = TRUE))    gammaformula = ~ 1,

The output is as follows:

  Call:
  colext(psiformula = ~0 + as.factor(PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND),

      gammaformula = ~1, epsilonformula = ~1, pformula = ~1, data = BLSA_UMF,
      control = list(trace = TRUE))

  Initial:
                                    Estimate      SE       z P(>|z|)

  as.factor(PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND)0  -19.442 759.470 -0.0256   0.980
  as.factor(PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND)1    0.159   0.309  0.5153   0.606

  Colonization:

   Estimate    SE     z  P(>|z|)

       -2.9 0.164 -17.6 1.03e-69

  Extinction:
   Estimate    SE     z  P(>|z|)

       -2.5 0.273 -9.18 4.12e-20

  Detection:
   Estimate     SE       z P(>|z|)

   -0.00962 0.0994 -0.0968   0.923

  AIC: 1435.122

The value -19.442 should be interpreted as "minus infinity" as that is the maximum likelihood estimate
of the logit of psi1 when PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND = 0 which corresponds to an
estimate of zero for psi1.

The value 0.159 is the estimate of the logit of psi1 when PRESENCE_WOODED_WETLAND = 1
which corresponds to an estimate of 0.5396665.

Jim

