Ah, sorry I don't think I explained my data properly.

I do have repeated measurements, just not on consecutive years. For example, site ETH_A_101 was surveyed in 2009, then skipped in 2010, then again in 2011. Site IND_C_101 was surveyed in 2010, skipped in 2011, then surveyed again in 2012, etc. In my INP file, for those skipped years, I have NAs in the secondary site columns. Example below, where I have 4 secondary sampling periods (p1, p2, p3, p4) for each primary sampling period (2009, 2010, etc). I've been able to consistently get parameter estimates with interpretable standard errors.

On Friday, August 31, 2018 at 2:19:01 AM UTC-5, Kery Marc wrote:
> Hi John,
>
> indeed, the dynamic multi-season occupancy model does not assume closure. But in order to ESTIMATE the parameters that describe this lack of closure (= colonization and extinction/persistence rates), you need repeated measurements from the same sites. These parameters are transition rates and you can only estimate them when you can observe (some of) these transitions. Since you don't observe a single transition (all change is due to the change in the identity of the sampled sites), you can't estimate the parameters under the dynamic model and are restricted to inferences under a static multi-season model as Marc explains.
>
> Best regards  ---- Marc
